      "title": "Use INT_MIN instead of -2147483648.",
      "message": "Use INT_MIN instead of -2147483648.\n\nFun fact: -2147483648 isn't an \"integer-constant\" in C, it's an\nexpression that applies the unary minus operator to the integer-constant -\n2147483648.  The Visual Studio C compiler, at least, treats 2147483648\nas an *unsigned* integer constant and warns about applying the unary\nminus operator to it.\n\nWhat we want is the smallest possible int value (which can't be validly\nnegated on two's-complement platforms), so just use INT_MIN and rely on\nthe compiler+header supplier to figure out how to define INT_MIN so as\nnot to provoke a compiler warning.  (At least some versions of Visual\nStudio define it as (-2147483647 - 1).)\n\nFixes #19340.\n",
